\description{
Spearman's rank correlation of every feature of a feature-by-sample table with one per-sample trait, with a two-sided p-value, optionally partial given per-sample covariates.

For each feature (row) of the responses table, mzLib's \code{SpearmanCorrelation.Correlate} ranks
the feature's values and the trait over the samples where both are known, and tests rho = 0,
two-sided. With \code{given}, \code{SpearmanCorrelation.PartialCorrelate} first removes from both sets of
ranks what the covariates' ranks explain, as \code{ppcor::pcor.test(method = "spearman")} does. mzLib
holds both to R's \code{cor.test()} and to ppcor 1.1 to 1e-8 relative.
}
\details{
Spearman uses ranks only. It needs no log transform, one extreme value cannot drive it, and it
finds any relationship that only ever rises (or only ever falls), not just a straight line.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_correlations}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per feature in the responses file's order: \code{feature};
    \code{rho}, Spearman's rho (unitless correlation, -1 to 1), ties at their average rank, the partial rho with
    \code{given}; \code{p_value}, a fraction (0 to 1), two-sided against rho = 0 and \strong{not adjusted across
    features} - both \code{NA} exactly when \code{method} is \code{"not_estimable"}; \code{n}, the complete samples
    used (samples: response, trait and every covariate finite); \code{has_ties}, whether any variable
    was tied among them (\code{FALSE} too when there were too few to rank); and \code{method}, \code{"exact"}
    (n <= 9, no ties, plain), \code{"asymptotic"} (t on n - 2 df, or n - 2 - k with k covariates kept)
    or \code{"not_estimable"}.
  - \code{responses_file}, \code{trait_file} and \code{given_file} (\code{NA} without \code{given}), as absolute paths;
    \code{trait_name}, the trait column's header; \code{covariate_names} (empty without \code{given});
    \code{partial}, \code{TRUE} with \code{given}; \code{feature_count} features and \code{sample_count} samples read;
    \code{sample_names}; \code{missing_count} response cells (cells) that were blank, \code{NA} or \code{NaN};
    \code{trait_missing_count}, samples with no trait value (samples); \code{given_incomplete_count},
    samples missing a covariate (samples), \code{NA} without \code{given}; \code{method_counts}, features per
    method; \code{row_count} rows; \code{column_names}.
  - \code{caveats}: how the p-values were computed and how they differ from R's defaults, plus a count
    of not_estimable features when there are any.

}
\section{Against R's cor.test()}{


For 10 <= n < 1290 without ties, R's \code{cor.test()} default is an Edgeworth series (AS 89), which
mzLib does not use, so p-values there differ slightly from R's default output; they match
\code{cor.test(exact = FALSE)}. Send \code{records$p_value} to \code{\link{stats_adjust}} for Benjamini-Hochberg.

}
