\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{responses}}{path; required. stats fit's responses table. A TSV with a header row: the first column is the feature id (unique, any header) and every other column is one sample, named by its header. A blank cell, NA or NaN (any case) is missing. Only ranks are used, so a monotone transform such as log2 changes nothing; but a 0 is an observation, ranked like any other value, so blank any 0 that means not measured. Blank lines are skipped.}
\item{\code{trait}}{path; required. A TSV with a header row and one row per sample: the sample name, then EXACTLY ONE value column, whose header names the trait (for example age or dose). The sample names must be exactly the responses' sample columns, in any order: rows are matched by name. A blank, NA or NaN cell is a sample with no trait value, left out of every feature's correlation.}
\item{\code{given}}{path; default absent. Optional. A TSV with the trait file's shape but one or more covariate columns, each named by its header: per-sample nuisance variables (batch, run order, loading) to partial out. With it, every feature is correlated by PartialCorrelate instead of Correlate. The sample names must be exactly the responses' sample columns; a blank, NA or NaN cell leaves that sample out of every feature's correlation. Absent, the bridge sends none and the correlation is plain.}
}
}
