\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item Plain: p_value is exact, from the full permutation distribution, for n <= 9 without ties, as R's cor.test(method = 'spearman'); otherwise it is asymptotic, t with n - 2 degrees of freedom, as cor.test(exact = FALSE).
\item Plain: for 10 <= n < 1290 without ties, R's cor.test default is an Edgeworth series (AS 89), which mzLib does not use, so p-values there differ slightly from R's default output.
\item With \code{--given}: every variable is ranked over the samples complete in all of them, rho is the correlation of the feature's and the trait's ranks after each is regressed on the covariates' ranks, and p_value is from t with n - 2 - k degrees of freedom, as ppcor::pcor.test(method = 'spearman'). mzLib drops a covariate that the others carry entirely, and k counts the covariates kept; mzLib does not report k, so neither does this verb.
\item With \code{--given}: give only nuisance covariates. A covariate that follows the trait takes the trait's own signal with it.
\item A sample enters a feature's correlation only when its response, its trait and every covariate are finite: missing values are omitted, never imputed. n is the number of samples used.
\item p_value is not adjusted across features. Send the p_value column to stats adjust for Benjamini-Hochberg.
}
}
